      "text": "To find the greatest possible three-digit number that is divisible by both 3 and 6, we need to understand the properties of these numbers. A number that is divisible by 6 must be divisible by both 2 and 3. Since all multiples of 6 are even, and every even number is divisible by 2, a number that is divisible by both 2 and 3 must be a multiple of their least common multiple (LCM), which is 6.\n\nTherefore, we need to find the greatest three-digit multiple of 6. The greatest three-digit number is 999. We divide 999 by 6 to find the largest integer quotient:\n\n\\[ 999 \\div 6 = 166.5 \\]\n\nThe integer part of the quotient is 166, so the greatest three-digit multiple of 6 is:\n\n\\[ 6 \\times 166 = 996 \\]\n\nTo confirm, we can check that 996 is divisible by both 3 and 6. Since 996 is an even number (it ends in 6), it is divisible by 2. Additionally, the sum of the digits of 996 is:\n\n\\[ 9 + 9 + 6 = 24 \\]\n\nSince 24 is divisible by 3, 996 is also divisible by 3. Therefore, 996 is indeed divisible by both 3 and 6. Thus, the greatest possible three-digit number that is divisible by both 3 and 6 is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{996} \\]",
